<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Filippo Juvarra</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1678">1678</a>–<a href="/years/1736">1736</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Filippo Juvarra was a Sicilian-born architect who became court architect to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-amadeus-ii">Victor Amadeus II</a> of Savoy in 1714. In and around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> he designed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basilica-of-superga">Basilica of Superga</a>, the façade of Palazzo Madama and the hunting lodge of Stupinigi, and worked on the Venaria Reale, making Turin a leading centre of late baroque architecture. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> while designing a new royal palace for Philip V.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/filippo-juvarra.jpg" alt="Filippo Juvarra" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Agostino Masucci · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Masucci-juvarra-acad.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
